  "reason": "F14 has measured native argument-normalization gains with no confirmed loss. One ordinary path costs 0.05258 -> 0.03102 us (-40.99%) for a short path and 0.05105 -> 0.03348 us (-34.41%) for a long path. 64 ordinary paths improve 87.25-87.55%; 512 short paths improve 12.78056 -> 1.33663 us (-89.54%) and long paths improve 89.44%. Raw allocations fall 70.004 -> 6.001 per 64-path call and 521.037 -> 9.003 per 512-path call, removing one temporary per ordinary argument. No-path control time is neutral (-0.62%) and allocations remain 2.000. All candidate observations retain exact leading/trailing/whitespace-only paths and order. These changed-output cases are diagnostic; their speed differences are not used as same-work performance gains.",
  "limits": "Thirteen profiles cover zero, one, 64 and 512 arguments with short, long and whitespace-heavy paths. All received three paired rounds plus AA/BB controls for runtime and raw allocations. Each many-path array repeats one prepared path; this is the isolated function cost, not whole CLI or file I/O. The legacy executable still has no moon.pkg and stale imports; the exact production function was extracted verbatim into white-box files, following the retained verification helper. The four existing path regression tests are copied unchanged into that package for the native gate. Native process peaks are within startup/control variation; no logical-live-memory claim. Raw allocation counts include amortized fixture/runner work.",
  "protocol": "Three paired rounds plus AA/BB controls for native runtime and raw allocations.",
  "thermal_note": "All 260 accepted samples started at or below 65 C; observed peak was at most 78.375 C. No sample was paused or discarded.",
